{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said, etc.</b> The statement here comes to prevent Moses from praying for Israel, just as He said to him in the affair of the calf (Exodus 32:10): ״הַנִּיחָה לִּי״. So too in this affair He prefaced by saying to him the matter stated below, so that he would refrain from praying for them — and therefore He did not say ״לֵאמֹר״." "<b>How long will they spurn Me, etc., and how long will they not believe, etc.</b> It is difficult: if they are spurning, what room is there to rage against the lack of faith? Perhaps it is against the spies that He said ״יְנַאֲצֻנִי״, and against the fact that the people believed them that He said ״לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ בִי״ — that I promised to give them the land:" "<b>Further</b>, on the grounds that there was in Israel one faction of scorners who acted wickedly and heretically — and according to their words, may their memory be for blessing, who said (Sotah 35a) concerning the verse ״לִרְגּוֹם אוֹתָם בָּאֲבָנִים וּכְבוֹד ה׳״ etc., that they were throwing stones even on high — against them He said ״יְנַאֲצֻנִי״. And there was one faction who were frightened by the nations, and against them He said ״לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ״. And against both aspects He said ״אַכֶּנּוּ בַדֶּבֶר וְאוֹרִישֶׁנּוּ״: against those who did not believe He said <b>אַכֶּנּוּ בַדֶּבֶר</b>, and He intended to bring an end, against the scorners, with <b>וְאוֹרִישֶׁנּוּ</b> — meaning, from the World to Come. And these two decrees were fulfilled in the spies: HaShem struck them with the plague, as it is written (Numbers 14:37): ״וַיָּמֻתוּ הָאֲנָשִׁים וְגוֹ׳ בַמַּגֵּפָה״, and they have no share in the World to Come, according to the statement of the Sages, may their memory be for blessing (Sanhedrin 108a)." "And His saying <b>ואעשה אותך</b> ('and I will make you') means — He will cause to flow into him and into his branches all the sparks of holiness that are fit to come, what was not in his power before. And His saying <b>ממנו</b> ('from you') means — that the mighty nation will be from the people mentioned at the beginning, that HaShem will incline all the souls dependent on that people and implant them in the root of the soul of Moses." "<b>Further</b>, the verses may be explained in this manner: it is because the affair of the calf had already preceded, and HaShem's statement there (Exodus 32:10) and Moses' reply in which he defeated the judgment and annulled the decree with the arguments stated there — on account of this, Elohim came to speak against those earlier arguments, and began by saying <b>עַד אָנָה</b>, meaning: there is no limit to the scorning of these people; say that this should be passed over for them and the power of forbearance is exhausted. And through this He also rejected Moses' argument that the Egyptians would say He brought them out in evil — for that would be the scorn of the nations once, whereas the scorn of Israel has no limit. Through this He also rejected the argument of the merit of the fathers, for it is not fitting for a great and exalted King, blessed is He, to endure so much scorn from among them." "<b>And you will find</b> that our Sages said (Shabbat 89b) that the patriarchs answered the Holy One, blessed be He, when He said to them ״Your children have sinned,״ they said to Him, ״Let them be blotted out for the sake of the honor of Your name,״ for they too did not desire the provocation of HaShem, blessed be He. And corresponding to the argument of ״whom You brought out of the land of Egypt״ that Moses said in the matter of the calf — which was intended to mean that a nation that would emerge from him to bear the yoke of divinity would not be equivalent to the people that came out of Egypt, upon whom the great signs and wonders were performed — for this reason He said that what they saw in the Exodus from Egypt availed them nothing, and this is His saying <b>and how long will they not believe in Me, despite all the signs</b> etc. And from now on there is no distinction for them on this account; if so, ״I shall strike them״ etc. And if you will say: but what shall I do about the oath to the patriarchs? — <b>and I will make you</b>, for you are of their descendants, <b>into a great nation</b> etc. Yet the difficulty remains: and how can you be assured of those who will come after them, that they too will not blaspheme? For this He said <b>and I will make</b> etc. <b>into a great and mighty nation</b> — meaning that the souls will be greater and mightier than theirs, meaning than the people now present, that they will be closer to holiness more than that people, so that they will be able to stand in the commandments of HaShem. And you will find that through the excellence of a holy soul it is facilitated to do holy deeds and to abhor doing evil; and see what I explained in the verse (Genesis 49:3) ״Reuben, you are my firstborn״ etc. And the people that came out of Egypt — because they were within the shell (kelippah) of Egypt and the garment of their captivity, they were not purified from it utterly, and this is a cause for blasphemy — which would not be the case with the people that would emerge from Moses."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {2 "The Talmudic citations are given in standard page notation: Sotah 35a and Sanhedrin 108a."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:16.403632115Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}